Z||000002||DaRkLoRD||05-20-2000||09:04 PM||||What about the mouldings and ground effects, if you wanted to put an aero nose onto a coupe body?<P>what do you have to replace on the existing trim?||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||05-20-2000||09:58 PM||||An exact replacement will need to come off of any '84-'86 coupe bodied Fiero....these have the black bumper pad things. <P>You can, as others have stated, put any Fiero bumper cover on, but you will need the inner fender wells that go with that particular bumper cover.....they are unique to each style.<P>The moldings might not match, so you'd need to convert to a round style molding if you used the '87-'88 coupe bumpers or '86-'88 GT bumpers.<P>||||reg||6
Z||000004||DaRkLoRD||05-20-2000||10:04 PM||||That's what I thought.<P>hmm. I was wondering the same thing last week, when I was checking out the prices of some 84 coupes in auto trader. I don't care much for the early coupe nose, but if the aero nose can be added, along with the matching trim and ground effects.. (and wing!) it'd be a cheap way of getting a fiero that looked like a later model..||||reg||1
Z||000005||Oreif||05-20-2000||10:55 PM||||If you want to go to the aero nose just get an 85 GT or 86/87 SE nose. They use the ribbed molding. The 86-88 GT and 87/88 coupes used smooth molding.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||05-21-2000||01:02 PM||||'84 Indy has the ribbed moulding, too.  (I know that you knew Oreif, but didn't know if everyone else did <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.)||||reg||1
